--TEST--
`net.internal` excepts the one address it names from `rule:security/net-address-policy`'s denied table and nothing else — `rule:http-server/allow-url-pins-the-address` and `rule:security/net-address-policy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3's operator half, which is the other side of
// `a-granted-host-is-still-refused-at-a-denied-address`: a deployment that must
// reach an internal API says so in `nvs.toml`, by **address** rather than by
// name, because the policy is asked of the resolved address and a name can
// resolve somewhere else after the check.
Core\Http\Target $local = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://127.0.0.1:8099/health");
echo "pinned: excepted address", "\n";

// The exception is one address, not a switch on the table. This second host is
// granted by `net.connect` exactly as the first one is, and its address is
// inside the same denied set, and it is still refused — so what the deployment
// bought back is legible as the one line that names it.
try {
    var $other = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://10.0.0.7/admin");
    echo "accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Two names granted, one address excepted. `net.internal` grants nothing on its
# own: an address named here stays unreachable until `connect` names the host as
# well, which is why both keys are written for the first line to work at all.
[capabilities.net]
connect = ["127.0.0.1", "10.0.0.7"]
internal = ["127.0.0.1"]
--EXPECT--
pinned: excepted address
Core\Http::allowUrl refuses 10.0.0.7: it is private (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16), which `net.internal` does not except
